
package Libraries.Compute.Noise

use Libraries.Compute.Math

/*
The NoiseGenerator class is the parent of classes that produce coherent noise: numbers that
change smoothly from place to place rather than jumping about, so that positions near each
other give values near each other.

A class inheriting from this one supplies GetNoise, which answers the noise at a position,
and receives from here the ways of combining that noise across several scales at once. Those
combinations turn a single smooth wobble into something with both broad shape and fine
detail, and they are written in terms of GetNoise alone, so any noise placed under this class
gains them without further work.

There is one rule an implementation has to obey. GetNoise must be a pure function of its
arguments: the same position gives the same answer every time it is asked, whatever was asked
before it. A class that draws numbers from a random sequence while it samples breaks that
rule, and with it every use that depends on two separate pieces of work agreeing about the
same place, such as two terrain tiles meeting along a shared edge. Randomness belongs in
Reseed, which runs once, and not in sampling, which runs everywhere.

Every action here answers between -1 and 1. Use ToUnitRange to move an answer to between
0 and 1 when something downstream wants it that way.

Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Compute.Noise.PerlinNoise

    class Main
        action Main
            PerlinNoise noise
            noise:SetSeed(1979)
            noise:SetFrequency(0.01)
            noise:SetOctaves(5)

            number value = noise:GetAdditiveNoise(120, 340)
        end
    end
*/
class NoiseGenerator
    Math math

    number seed = 0

    /*How much of the noise fits into a unit of distance. Small values give broad, slow
    features and large values give tight, busy ones.*/
    number frequency = 0.01

    /*How many copies of the noise are combined, each finer than the last.*/
    integer octaves = 4

    /*How much the frequency rises from one copy to the next. Two is usual, meaning each
    copy has features half the size of the one before it.*/
    number lacunarity = 2

    /*How much of its height each copy keeps compared to the one before it. A half is usual,
    so that the finer copies roughen the shape without burying it.*/
    number persistence = 0.5

    /*How strongly ridged noise gathers its detail onto the crests. Used by nothing else.*/
    number ridgeGain = 2
    
    
    /*How far the fold sits from zero. One is usual and puts the crease exactly where the
    noise changes sign.*/
    number ridgeOffset = 1

    /*How freely an octave hands its allowance to the next, and how that allowance is bent
    before it is used. The page these come from fixes them at one and a half and four fifths.*/
    number ridgeWeightGain = 1.5
    number ridgeWeightExponent = 0.8
    
    /*How hard steep ground is pushed toward the middle. Near a twentieth leaves rolling
    country; near a half cuts the steep parts away sharply.*/
    number slopeAlpha = 0.15

    /*How far apart the two samples are that the slope is measured across.*/
    number slopeSpacing = 1
    
    /*How coarse the noise that bends the coordinates is. Below the surface frequency it
    gives broad sweeping bends; above it the surface turns jittery.*/
    number warpFrequency = 0.004

    /*How far the coordinates are bent, in the same units positions are given in. At zero the
    surface is left exactly as it was.*/
    number warpStrength = 40

    /*
    This action returns the noise at a position, between -1 and 1.

    Every class inheriting from NoiseGenerator supplies this action, and it has to be a pure
    function of its two arguments. The frequency is not applied here: this action answers the
    noise at exactly the position given, and the combining actions below scale positions
    before they call it.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The noise at that position, between -1 and 1.
    */
    blueprint action GetNoise(number x, number y) returns number

    /*
    This action rebuilds whatever an implementation derives from the seed, such as a table of
    shuffled numbers. It runs when the seed changes, and it is the only place an
    implementation may draw from a random sequence.
    */
    blueprint action Reseed

    /*
    This action adds several copies of the noise together, which is often called fractal
    noise. Each copy is sampled at a higher frequency and a smaller height than the one
    before, so the first gives the broad shape of the surface and the later ones roughen it.

    The total is divided by the combined height of all the copies, so the answer stays
    between -1 and 1 however many are used.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The combined noise, between -1 and 1.
    */
    action GetAdditiveNoise(number x, number y) returns number
        number total = 0
        number reach = 0
        number amplitude = 1
        number currentFrequency = frequency

        integer i = 0
        repeat while i < octaves
            total = total + GetNoise(x * currentFrequency, y * currentFrequency) * amplitude
            reach = reach + amplitude
            amplitude = amplitude * persistence
            currentFrequency = currentFrequency * lacunarity
            i = i + 1
        end

        if reach = 0
            return 0
        end

        return total / reach
    end

    /*
    This action multiplies several copies of the noise together instead of adding them. Each
    copy is moved to between 0 and 1 first, so that the product stays there, and the answer is
    moved back to between -1 and 1 at the end to match the rest of this class.

    Because numbers below one shrink when multiplied, the answer spends most of its time near
    the bottom of its range and rises only where every copy happens to be high at the same
    place. Used for terrain that reads as broad low ground broken by occasional high ground,
    where additive noise gives rolling country everywhere instead.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The combined noise, between -1 and 1.
    */
    action GetMultiplicativeNoise(number x, number y) returns number
        number total = 1
        number currentFrequency = frequency

        integer i = 0
        repeat while i < octaves
            number sample = (GetNoise(x * currentFrequency, y * currentFrequency) + 1) / 2
            total = total * sample
            currentFrequency = currentFrequency * lacunarity
            i = i + 1
        end

        return total * 2 - 1
    end

    /*
    This action folds the noise about zero before adding its octaves together, which turns
    the places where the noise crossed zero into sharp crests instead of gentle slopes.

    Additive noise wanders smoothly through zero and gives rounded hills. Taking the distance
    from zero instead, and turning it upside down, leaves a value that is highest exactly
    where the noise changed sign. Folding a smooth curve that way puts a corner in it, and it
    is that corner, repeated at every scale, that reads as a ridge line. The result is squared
    afterwards to draw the crests finer and settle the ground on either side of them.

    Each octave is also held back by how high the octave before it stood, so that fine detail
    gathers along the crests while the low ground between them stays smooth. Without that,
    every scale ridges everywhere at once and the result reads as crumpled paper rather than
    as mountains. How sharply detail is gathered is set by SetRidgeGain.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The combined noise, between -1 and 1.
    */
    action GetRidgedNoise(number x, number y) returns number
        number total = 0
        number reach = 0
        number amplitude = 1
        number currentFrequency = frequency

        //The first octave stands on its own. Every one after it is held back by the last.
        number weight = 1

        integer i = 0
        repeat while i < octaves
            number folded = 1 - math:AbsoluteValue(GetNoise(x * currentFrequency, y * currentFrequency))
            number signal = folded * folded * weight

            //Carry the height of this octave forward as the allowance for the next one,
            //kept between nothing and all of it so that a loud octave cannot let the one
            //after it push the total past the range the shares below are counting on.
            weight = signal * ridgeGain

            if weight < 0
                weight = 0
            end

            if weight > 1
                weight = 1
            end

            total = total + signal * amplitude
            reach = reach + amplitude
            amplitude = amplitude * persistence
            currentFrequency = currentFrequency * lacunarity
            i = i + 1
        end

        if reach = 0
            return 0
        end

        //Every octave landed between 0 and 1, so dividing by their combined height lands the
        //total there too, and the last step moves it to the range the rest of this class uses.
        return (total / reach) * 2 - 1
    end
    

    /*
    This action is the body of additive noise with the starting frequency handed in rather
    than taken from the field, so that domain warping can raise its offsets at a scale of
    their own without disturbing the scale the surface itself is built at.
    */
    private action AddOctaves(number x, number y, number startFrequency) returns number
        number total = 0
        number reach = 0
        number amplitude = 1
        number currentFrequency = startFrequency

        integer i = 0
        repeat while i < octaves
            total = total + GetNoise(x * currentFrequency, y * currentFrequency) * amplitude
            reach = reach + amplitude
            amplitude = amplitude * persistence
            currentFrequency = currentFrequency * lacunarity
            i = i + 1
        end

        if reach = 0
            return 0
        end

        return total / reach
    end
    
    
    /*
    This action bends a position sideways before the surface is sampled at it, which is what
    turns the even, soapy look of plain fractal noise into something with the folded, dragged
    quality of real ground. Nothing about the surface changes; it is asked about somewhere
    slightly different, and how far it is dragged is itself decided by noise.

    Use it by bending a position and sampling with the result, which works with any of the
    ways of combining octaves rather than only one:

        number bentX = noise:GetWarpedX(x, y)
        number bentY = noise:GetWarpedY(x, y)
        number height = noise:GetRidgedNoise(bentX, bentY)

    The bend is worked out from the position alone, so a bent surface is still the same
    everywhere it is asked about, and two terrain tiles sharing an edge still meet along it.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The first axis of the bent position.
    */
    action GetWarpedX(number x, number y) returns number
        return x + AddOctaves(x + 1731.7, y + 4021.3, warpFrequency) * warpStrength
    end
    
    
    /*
    This action bends the second axis of a position. See GetWarpedX.

    The far apart sampling positions here and in GetWarpedX are what keep the two bends from
    resembling one another. Sampled at the same place they would move every position along a
    single diagonal instead of in a swirl.
    */
    action GetWarpedY(number x, number y) returns number
        return y + AddOctaves(x + 6803.1, y + 2417.9, warpFrequency) * warpStrength
    end
    
    
    /*
    This action pushes a height toward the middle of its range in proportion to how steep the
    ground is there, so that gentle country is left alone and cliffs are worn down. The
    steepness is the length of the slope taken in both directions at once, and the height is
    multiplied by a number that falls away as that steepness grows.

    Attribute: Parameter height The height at the position.
    Attribute: Parameter slopeX How fast the height is changing along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter slopeY How fast the height is changing along the second axis.
    */
    action WeightBySlope(number height, number slopeX, number slopeY) returns number
        number slope = math:SquareRoot(slopeX * slopeX + slopeY * slopeY)
        return height * math:RaiseToPower(math:e, -slopeAlpha * slope)
    end

    /*
    This action measures the slope of additive noise and wears the steep parts of it down.

    The slope is taken across the position rather than from it, a step either way along each
    axis, so that the answer does not lean toward one side. That costs five samples of the
    surface where an unweighted height costs one, so it is the most expensive thing in this
    class by some way.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The worn down height, between -1 and 1.
    */
    action GetSlopeWeightedNoise(number x, number y) returns number
        number here = GetAdditiveNoise(x, y)

        number slopeX = (GetAdditiveNoise(x + slopeSpacing, y) - GetAdditiveNoise(x - slopeSpacing, y)) / (2 * slopeSpacing)
        number slopeY = (GetAdditiveNoise(x, y + slopeSpacing) - GetAdditiveNoise(x, y - slopeSpacing)) / (2 * slopeSpacing)

        return WeightBySlope(here, slopeX, slopeY)
    end

    action GetSlopeAlpha returns number
        return slopeAlpha
    end

    action SetSlopeAlpha(number slopeAlpha)
        me:slopeAlpha = slopeAlpha
    end

    action GetSlopeSpacing returns number
        return slopeSpacing
    end

    action SetSlopeSpacing(number slopeSpacing)
        me:slopeSpacing = slopeSpacing
    end

    

    action GetWarpFrequency returns number
        return warpFrequency
    end

    action SetWarpFrequency(number warpFrequency)
        me:warpFrequency = warpFrequency
    end

    action GetWarpStrength returns number
        return warpStrength
    end

    action SetWarpStrength(number warpStrength)
        me:warpStrength = warpStrength
    end
    
    
    /*
    This action combines octaves the way a ridged multifractal does, folding the noise about
    an offset, squaring what is left, and turning it upside down, so that the crease the fold
    puts in at the noise's zero crossings becomes the floor of a sharp valley rather than the
    top of a ridge. Ground between the valleys is left smooth.

    That is the opposite way up from GetRidgedNoise, which puts crests at the crossings
    instead. The two are worth thinking of as a pair: one cuts canyons into high ground, the
    other raises ridges out of low ground.

    Each octave is also held back by how high the one before it stood, bent by an exponent so
    that the falling off is gradual rather than abrupt, which gathers fine detail near the
    valley walls and leaves the ground between them smooth.

    Attribute: Parameter x The position along the first axis.
    Attribute: Parameter y The position along the second axis.
    Attribute: Returns The combined noise, between -1 and 1.
    */
    action GetRidgedMultifractalNoise(number x, number y) returns number
        number total = 0
        number reach = 0
        number amplitude = 1
        number currentFrequency = frequency

        //The first octave stands on its own. Every one after it is held back by the last.
        number weight = 1

        integer i = 0
        repeat while i < octaves
            number folded = ridgeOffset - math:AbsoluteValue(GetNoise(x * currentFrequency, y * currentFrequency))
            number ridge = 1 - folded * folded

            //An offset away from one can push this below nothing, which would let an octave
            //subtract from the total instead of adding to it.
            if ridge < 0
                ridge = 0
            end

            total = total + ridge * amplitude * weight
            reach = reach + amplitude

            number carried = ridge * ridgeWeightGain

            if carried < 0
                carried = 0
            end

            if carried > 1
                carried = 1
            end

            weight = math:RaiseToPower(carried, ridgeWeightExponent)

            amplitude = amplitude * persistence
            currentFrequency = currentFrequency * lacunarity
            i = i + 1
        end

        if reach = 0
            return 0
        end

        return (total / reach) * 2 - 1
    end

    /*
    This action moves a value from between -1 and 1 to between 0 and 1, which is what things
    like height maps expect. Values that stray outside the range are brought back to its edge
    rather than allowed through, since a height above the top of a map wraps around into a
    cliff.

    Attribute: Parameter value A value between -1 and 1.
    Attribute: Returns The same value expressed between 0 and 1.
    */
    action ToUnitRange(number value) returns number
        number result = (value + 1) / 2

        if result < 0
            result = 0
        end

        if result > 1
            result = 1
        end

        return result
    end

    action GetSeed returns number
        return seed
    end

    /*
    This action sets which noise gets generated. The same seed always gives the same noise,
    so a world can be thrown away and rebuilt from its seed alone.

    Attribute: Parameter seed The number that decides the noise.
    */
    action SetSeed(number seed)
        me:seed = seed
        Reseed()
    end

    action GetFrequency returns number
        return frequency
    end

    /*
    This action sets how much of the noise fits into a unit of distance. A frequency of a
    hundredth means the noise takes about a hundred units to complete a feature.

    Take care not to set this to a whole number while sampling at whole positions. Perlin
    noise is exactly zero wherever its lattice crosses, and a frequency of one puts every
    whole position on a crossing, so a height map sampled that way comes out perfectly flat.

    Attribute: Parameter frequency How much noise fits into a unit of distance.
    */
    action SetFrequency(number frequency)
        me:frequency = frequency
    end

    action GetOctaves returns integer
        return octaves
    end

    /*
    This action sets how many copies of the noise are combined. Each copy costs another
    sample, so the work grows in step with this number, and copies finer than the spacing of
    whatever is being filled in add cost without adding anything anyone can see.

    Attribute: Parameter octaves How many copies to combine, at least one.
    */
    action SetOctaves(integer octaves)
        if octaves < 1
            octaves = 1
        end

        me:octaves = octaves
    end

    action GetLacunarity returns number
        return lacunarity
    end

    /*
    This action sets how much the frequency rises from one copy of the noise to the next.

    Attribute: Parameter lacunarity The rise in frequency per copy, usually two.
    */
    action SetLacunarity(number lacunarity)
        me:lacunarity = lacunarity
    end

    action GetPersistence returns number
        return persistence
    end

    /*
    This action sets how much of its height each copy of the noise keeps compared to the one
    before it. Below a half the shape stays smooth, and above it the fine copies begin to
    dominate and the result turns rough.

    Attribute: Parameter persistence The share of height kept per copy, usually a half.
    */
    action SetPersistence(number persistence)
        me:persistence = persistence
    end

    action GetRidgeGain returns number
        return ridgeGain
    end

    /*
    This action sets how strongly ridged noise gathers its detail onto the crests, and has no
    effect on any of the other ways of combining octaves.

    At one, an octave passes on exactly as much allowance as it stood tall, and detail thins
    out gently down the flanks. Above that, allowance is granted more freely, so detail
    reaches further down and the whole surface roughens. At zero, every octave after the first
    is silenced and only the broadest ridge remains.

    Attribute: Parameter ridgeGain How freely detail is granted to the crests, usually two.
    */
    action SetRidgeGain(number ridgeGain)
        me:ridgeGain = ridgeGain
    end
end